Mild seasonal pruritus of the soft palate and ears associated with oral allergy syndrome
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Palatal and pharyngeal pruritus, Ear canal itching, Triggered by raw produce

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-021
Reason for Visit: Rapid onset of oropharyngeal swelling sensation and severe itching requiring immediate assessment to exclude systemic anaphylaxis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden onset of intense oral and pharyngeal itching immediately following food ingestion. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required prior to the next in-hours period to directly evaluate airway patency, exclude impending glottic edema, and rule out systemic anaphylactic shock.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My mouth and the inside of my ears started itching wildly the second I bit into that raw apple.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a rapid onset of transient pruritus of the soft palate and posterior pharynx beginning within minutes of initial allergen contact. Describes a deep-seated itching sensation in the bilateral external auditory canals. Notes that the exacerbation is tightly correlated with specific raw fruit ingestion. Also mentions concomitant seasonal allergic rhinorrhea and sneezing over the past week. Reports a complete absence of visible oropharyngeal swelling, difficulty swallowing, or airway compromise. Spontaneous resolution of peak symptoms is underway without systemic manifestation.
Functional Status: Fully independent, speaking in full sentences.
Social/Safety Concerns: Anxiety regarding potential progression to severe food allergy.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 124/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, actively clearing throat from mild irritation but in no respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal examination reveals normal uvular position and no mucosal edema. Diagnostic triad confirmed: palatal and pharyngeal pruritus, ear canal itching, and a history triggered by raw produce. Normal bilateral otoscopic examination with no erythema or effusion. Pulmonary auscultation reveals clear bilateral breath sounds with zero wheezing or stridor. Cutaneous exam lacks generalized urticaria.
Environmental Observations: Half-eaten raw apple on the kitchen counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient takes over-the-counter cetirizine sporadically.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild seasonal pruritus of the soft palate and ears associated with oral allergy syndrome - Status: Improving
Differentials:

Systemic Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of airway swelling, normal hemodynamics, and lack of generalized cutaneous urticaria.

Acute Otitis Media - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal bilateral otoscopic examination and complete absence of tympanic membrane bulging or erythema.

Oral Candidiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sudden onset immediately following food ingestion and absence of white adherent mucosal plaques.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden development of severe oral and pharyngeal pruritus following food ingestion strongly mimics the prodrome of anaphylactic shock. Given the lethal potential of rapidly progressing glottic edema, clinical assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period. Immediate, face-to-face evaluation confirmed an intact, uncompromised airway and restricted the symptoms to a localized cross-reactive pollen-food syndrome, thereby preventing an unnecessary emergency department transfer while ensuring patient safety.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance. Recommendation for an immediate dose of oral non-sedating antihistamine. Planned non-urgent outpatient testing for specific IgE cross-reactive pollen allergens.
Positive Behavioral Change: Microwave or bake raw fruits for 30 seconds before eating to thermally denature the offending cross-reactive pollen proteins, linking the safe consumption of the food to the physical cue of using the kitchen appliance.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if symptoms fail to resolve or if new foods trigger identical reactions.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop sudden difficulty breathing, voice changes, swelling of the lips or tongue, or a widespread hive-like rash across your body.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis assumes a localized allergic response; future reactions can occasionally be unpredictable.
Clinical Course: The oral itching typically resolves within 30 to 60 minutes, especially after the offending food is swallowed or removed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you feel a lump in your throat that prevents swallowing, or if you become dizzy and lightheaded.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your body is mistaking the proteins in the raw apple for the tree pollen you are already allergic to. Cooking the fruit changes the protein shape so your immune system ignores it.
Actionable Steps: Take your daily antihistamine during pollen season. Avoid raw cross-reactive fruits; cook or peel them instead.

